Demand Forecasting Case Study

Eight-Week Ahead Demand Forecasting using Machine Learning

⸻

Business Problem

Retail organizations rely on accurate demand forecasts to optimize inventory and production planning. Poor forecasting can lead to:

* Frequent stock-outs of high-demand products.
* Excess inventory and increased holding costs.
* Inefficient production and replenishment planning.
* Higher inventory write-offs.

Objective: Develop a Machine Learning model to forecast product demand 8 weeks (56 days) ahead for each supermarket–SKU combination.

⸻

Approach

* Conducted Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) to identify demand patterns and data quality issues.
* Engineered calendar, promotion, lag, and rolling statistical features.
* Applied a chronological train-test split to preserve temporal order and prevent data leakage.
* Developed and evaluated multiple forecasting models:
    * Persistence Baseline
    * Linear Regression
    * Ridge Regression
    * Random Forest (Tuned)
    * XGBoost (Tuned)

⸻

Results

Metric	Ridge Regression
MAE	3.48
RMSE	14.64
R² Score	0.618
MAPE	5.25%

Final Model: Ridge Regression

Selected because it achieved the lowest RMSE, the highest R² score, and demonstrated strong generalization while maintaining a simple and interpretable model architecture.

⸻

Business Impact

The developed forecasting pipeline can support retail organizations by:

* Improving demand planning accuracy.
* Reducing stock-outs and excess inventory.
* Optimizing production and replenishment schedules.
* Lowering inventory holding costs.
* Supporting data-driven supply chain decisions.
* Enhancing overall operational efficiency.

⸻

Technology Stack

Python • Pandas • NumPy • Scikit-learn • XGBoost • Joblib • Jupyter Notebook